---
<Base
  title="Zero-Touch Network Device Provisioning Lab · San Lee"
  description="An end-to-end network automation lab takes a factory-blank MikroTik device to a checked configuration after one power cycle."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/netops-lab.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-netops-lab.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Wipe-to-provision topology (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: the
       argument is this lab's closed loop, not a shared diagram type.
       The box properties moved out to the figure ladder (ADR-014). All four
       artworks on this page are 260-unit drawings. They therefore take ONE
       cap, and the page states one drawn scale. The old max-width 260 pinned
       the scale at 1.000; the ladder hangs a plate at --fig-plate. Measured
       2026-08-30: 384px at 1280 (1.477x) and 272px at 320 (1.046x). The scale
       never drops under 1.0, so the microtext floor still reads off the
       declared unit. */
    .lab-topo .lt-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-id {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-role {
      fill: var(--text-secondary);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Sole human edge: solid accent, dual-encoded with a filled mark + BY HAND
       (matches .cycle [data-actor=hand] / .cycle-actor). Not oxide — oxide is
       reversal on this site. */
    .lab-topo .lt-hand {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-hand-mark { fill: var(--accent); stroke: none; }
    .lab-topo .lt-hand-label {
      fill: var(--accent);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-hand-noun {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
    }
    /* Return edge: re-arm closes the loop. Accent + dasharray like L4's
       be-back so it reads as the load-bearing cycle, not another cable. */
    .lab-topo .lt-return {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-note {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 12px;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-link-label {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 10px;
    }
    .lab-topo marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .lab-topo marker path.lt-head-hand { fill: var(--accent); }
    .lab-topo marker path.lt-head-return { fill: var(--accent); }
    /* The bare plate's what-slot. A div cannot carry a figcaption, so this
       caption stays page-scoped, exactly as .tt-plate-caption does on the
       telltale page. Its declarations now follow the consolidated caption
       grammar (ADR-014). The what-slot argues in the note voice, at the prose
       measure, centred under the artwork. */
    .lab-topo-caption {
      max-width: var(--content);
      margin: 14px auto 0;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      line-height: 1.55;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }

    /* The two-slot caption CSS that used to sit here is DELETED, not
       out-cascaded. style.css now holds the one home for the caption grammar
       (ADR-014, OC-F closed by lane I0). This page's local copy and its
       origin comment went with it.

       The artwork cap is what stays page-scoped. The ladder in style.css caps
       a plate through `.fig--plate > svg` and `figure[data-fig="plate"] >
       svg`. It says nothing about a map, because a map normally has an
       intrinsic width worth the whole sheet. The three maps here do not: they
       are 260-unit drawings authored to the same discipline as the plate
       beside them. So they take the plate cap and this page renders ONE drawn
       scale. `data-place` still follows the per-kind default, so the map
       takes the sheet track and the artwork centres in it. */
    .lab-topo,
    .nl1-doors,
    .nl3-devmode {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: min(100%, var(--fig-plate));
      height: auto;
      margin-left: auto;
      margin-right: auto;
    }

    /* The three new figures. Prefixes nl1-, nl2- and nl3- keep every
       marker id and class unique against the lt- prefix above, because a
       duplicate marker id repaints the wrong arrow head and no gate sees
       it. Two hues only: --accent marks the one side path that was open,
       and the one node no script can enter. --accent-warm means a
       reversal on this site, and nothing on this page is a reversal, so
       it does not appear here either (same rule as .lab-topo).
       Every state is dual-encoded by a word and a shape, because
       contrast-check.cjs exempts SVG text and no gate reads a plate.
       The 260-unit viewBox now hangs on the figure ladder rather than at
       1:1, so the microtext floor owes a measurement (CLAUDE.md). Measured
       2026-08-30 at a 320px viewport: scale 1.046, and the smallest text on
       this page renders at 10.46px against the 9px floor. */
    .nl1-doors .nl1-box,
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-row,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-name,
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-state,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-step {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
    }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-disp,
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-verdict,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-sub {
      fill: var(--text-secondary);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
    }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-group,
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-kicker {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-id,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-id {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-edge,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* A closed side path stops at a bar and never reaches the device. The
       bar is the shape half of the dual encoding; the disposition word
       under the name is the word half. */
    .nl1-doors .nl1-stop {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* The open path, and the hand node. Accent, a filled mark, and an
       uppercase word, exactly as .lt-hand / .lt-hand-mark /
       .lt-hand-label already encode the one hand in .lab-topo. */
    .nl1-doors .nl1-open,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-hand-box {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-open-mark,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-hand-mark { fill: var(--accent); stroke: none; }
    .nl1-doors .nl1-open-label,
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-mark-label,
    .nl3-devmode .nl3-hand-label {
      fill: var(--accent);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-dot {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-row-marked {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .nl2-hostkey .nl2-dot-marked { fill: var(--accent); stroke: none; }
    .nl1-doors marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .nl1-doors marker path.nl1-head-open { fill: var(--accent); }
    .nl3-devmode marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Zero-Touch Provisioning</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A factory-blank router configures itself on one power cycle. One command, about ninety seconds.</p>

    <p class="lead">
      This is a physical lab: one MikroTik router, one Raspberry Pi, on a desk.
      The goal was a reliable wipe-to-provisioned cycle. Power on a blank
      device, touch nothing, and it comes up fully configured. That cycle
      is the apparatus for a later experiment: an agent that severs its own
      management access. Without a cheap way back, every failed run is a
      manual rebuild.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" id="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <p class="toc-heading" id="toc-heading">On this page</p>
      {/* The Contents' figure entries (ADR-014): a quiet comma-run of the
          figure numbers each section holds. Plain text, never anchors, so a
          phone pays no 44px box per figure. */}
      <ol>
      <li><a href="#the-result">The result</a><span class="toc-figs">Map 01</span></li>
      <li><a href="#the-finding-the-barriers-were-on-the-host-not">The finding: the barriers were on the host, not the device</a><span class="toc-figs">Plate 02</span></li>
      <li><a href="#three-back-doors-one-shape">Three back doors, one shape</a><span class="toc-figs">Map 03</span></li>
      <li><a href="#a-deferred-item-that-turned-out-not-to-be">A deferred item that turned out not to be deferrable</a><span class="toc-figs">Map 04</span></li>
      <li><a href="#what-this-does-not-establish">What this does not establish</a></li>
      <li><a href="#stack">Stack</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-it-demonstrates">What it demonstrates</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <h2 id="the-result">The result</h2>
    <p>
      One command, one power cycle, about ninety seconds. No console cable, no
      serial adapter, no reset-button timing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since then the cycle shed its last hand
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/netops-lab/blob/main/decisions/008-unattended-wipe-cycle.md">decision 008</a>,
      2026-08-03). A repeat cycle issues that same reboot over SSH, so a routine
      wipe is one command with no physical step at all. The power pull survives
      in exactly two places: a factory-blank board&rsquo;s first boot, and a router
      that is already unreachable over its management path.
    </p>

    <!-- Topology of the wipe-to-provision lab. The ordered list below still
         carries the five steps; this figure's job is the closed loop a
         straight list cannot draw: re-arm returns into the next wipe, and
         power is the only hand in the apparatus. No 1–5 numbering here —
         sequence stays on ol.cycle (ADR-004 Decision 5 floor).
         A legacy bare plate takes a div wrapper, never a figure. That is gate
         law. It also takes a hand-numbered label span (PL-3, PL-4). The kind
         is Map: the drawing argues about a topology, not about a state set. The
         caption travels inside the wrapper so the artwork, its label, and its
         what-slot are one block under the rhythm ladder. -->
    <div class="fig" data-place="sheet">
    <span class="fig-label">Map 01</span>
    <svg class="lab-topo" viewBox="0 0 260 150" role="img"
         aria-label="Wipe-to-provision lab. The Raspberry Pi Netinstall host links to the router over an isolated Ethernet path for BOOTP and TFTP. The only human step is to power-cycle the router on a factory-blank first boot. The router’s final re-arm step closes the loop so the next wipe can start.">
      <title>Wipe-to-provision lab. The Raspberry Pi Netinstall host links to the router over an isolated Ethernet path for BOOTP and TFTP. The only human step is to power-cycle the router on a factory-blank first boot. The router’s final re-arm step closes the loop so the next wipe can start.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="lt-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="lt-arrow-start" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="1" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="lt-arrow-hand" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path class="lt-head-hand" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="lt-arrow-return" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path class="lt-head-return" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Sole human edge: Power → Router. Filled mark + BY HAND dual-encode
           actor=hand the same way the cycle list does. Labels sit left of the
           mark so the tracked uppercase does not overflow the 260 viewBox. -->
      <text class="lt-hand-noun" x="196" y="12" text-anchor="end">Power</text>
      <circle class="lt-hand-mark" cx="204" cy="10" r="4" />
      <path class="lt-hand" d="M 204 15 L 204 36" marker-end="url(#lt-arrow-hand)" />
      <text class="lt-hand-label" x="196" y="30" text-anchor="end">By hand</text>

      <!-- Two boxes only — schematic hosts, not photoreal hardware. rx matches
           --radius-sm (2px); at max-width 260 viewBox units are CSS px 1:1. -->
      <rect class="lt-box" x="6" y="40" width="100" height="48" rx="2" />
      <text class="lt-id" x="56" y="60" text-anchor="middle">Pi</text>
      <text class="lt-role" x="56" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Netinstall</text>

      <rect class="lt-box" x="154" y="40" width="100" height="48" rx="2" />
      <text class="lt-id" x="204" y="60" text-anchor="middle">Router</text>
      <text class="lt-role" x="204" y="76" text-anchor="middle">wipe &#8594; cfg</text>

      <!-- Isolated Ethernet: Pi ↔ Router carries BOOTP/TFTP. Muted automated
           stroke — not the hand edge, not the return. -->
      <path class="lt-edge" d="M 108 64 L 150 64"
            marker-start="url(#lt-arrow-start)"
            marker-end="url(#lt-arrow)" />
      <text class="lt-link-label" x="129" y="56" text-anchor="middle">BOOTP / TFTP</text>

      <!-- Return edge under the pair: router re-arm → next wipe on the Pi
           side. Same closed-loop pattern as split-map A-return / L4 be-back. -->
      <path class="lt-return" d="M 204 90 C 204 128, 56 128, 56 92"
            marker-end="url(#lt-arrow-return)" />
      <text class="lt-note" x="130" y="144" text-anchor="middle">re-arm closes the next wipe</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="lab-topo-caption">
      Power is the only hand in the loop, and only on a factory-blank first
      boot. Netinstall, the blank-router offer, the OS and default config, and
      the re-arm all run automatically. Re-arm is the return edge that starts
      the next wipe with no rebuild of the host. On repeat cycles even the hand
      is not needed: a repeat cycle issues the reboot over SSH.
    </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="cycle" aria-label="One wipe-to-provisioned cycle">
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">1</span>Start Netinstall on the Pi</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">It carries its own BOOTP and TFTP server, so nothing else has to run.</p>
      </li>
      <li data-actor="hand">
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">2</span>Pull the power, plug it back in<span class="cycle-actor">by hand</span></p>
        <p class="cycle-note">By hand only on a factory-blank first boot. A repeat cycle issues the same reboot over SSH, and Etherboot cannot tell the difference (decision 008).</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">3</span>The router offers itself, Netinstall answers</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">It tries Ethernet once on boot, then falls through to its own flash if nothing answers.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">4</span>Fresh OS, then my default configuration</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">A management address, a firewall, and an SSH key that it never saw before.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">5</span>It re-arms itself for the next run</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">The last statement in the config script, deliberately: if the router refuses it, everything else already applied.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="cycle-close">
      On a factory-blank first boot, exactly one of the five needs a person.
      That person pulls the power and plugs it back in. On every cycle after
      that, none of them do: the reboot goes over SSH and the whole loop is one
      command.
    </p>
    <p>
      The provisioning host is the Pi rather than my desktop, so the whole cycle
      is scriptable from the box that is already always-on. MikroTik&rsquo;s
      Netinstall tool ships as a 32-bit x86 binary, and the Pi is ARM, so it
      runs under user-mode QEMU. The binary is statically linked, so there is
      no 32-bit sysroot to assemble.
    </p>

    {/* The Contents return (ADR-014, PL-7, PL-8). The ToC gate carries this
        page on screens, not on figures. Measured 2026-08-30: 12.85 phone
        screens at 393px against the gate's floor of 8. The page holds four
        figures against the gate's other floor of six. The nav.toc above holds
        id="toc", so every target resolves on this page. The first section
        head takes no return: it sits about one screen under the Contents it
        would point at. */}
    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="the-finding-the-barriers-were-on-the-host-not">The finding: the barriers were on the host, not the device</h2>
    <p>
      After a fresh install, RouterOS leaves the admin account with a blank
      password. It demands that you change the password interactively before
      you do anything. That looks like it makes unattended provisioning
      impossible, and I already began the design around it. That design would
      need a credential in a public repository, or machinery that injects one
      at provision time.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">The fix was to test the assumption instead of designing around it</p>
      <p>Before building anything, I ran the one command that would tell me
        whether the problem was real in the case I cared about: a
        <em>non-interactive</em> SSH, the way a script would actually connect.</p>
      <p>It printed its output and exited. The forced password change is a
        property of interactive logins only. A script never meets it. The
        provisioning script needed no changes at all, and the credential problem
        I was about to solve did not exist.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What did block automation</strong> showed up in
        the output of that same test, in two lines I had been typing past all
        evening: an SSH host-key confirmation prompt, and a passphrase prompt on
        my own key. Neither stops a human. Both stop a script dead.</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The host-key prompt is not incidental, and it does not go away. Netinstall
      regenerates the router&rsquo;s SSH host keys on every cycle. The
      device&rsquo;s identity therefore changes every single time, legitimately
      and by design.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lazy fix is to disable host-key checking. That removes a real
      protection to silence a prompt. What the lab actually wants is a
      <em>separate</em> known-hosts file, so a provisioning script discards one
      file per cycle and touches nothing else. It still refuses a
      <em>changed</em> key mid-cycle, which is the case that would genuinely
      mean something is wrong.
    </p>

    <!-- Three states of one host key, with the discriminating case marked.
         The plate carries the set. The argument above it stays as prose: a
         disabled host-key check removes a real protection to silence a
         prompt. Accent, a filled mark, and the word REFUSE dual-encode the
         marked row, because contrast-check.cjs exempts SVG text and no gate
         reads a plate. No digit here, so the counts stay in the prose. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 02</span>
      <svg class="nl2-hostkey" viewBox="0 0 260 118" role="img"
           aria-label="Three states of the router SSH host key. A new key after a wipe is expected. The same key is known. A key that changed mid-cycle is refused.">
        <title>Three states of the router SSH host key. A new key after a wipe is expected. The same key is known. A key that changed mid-cycle is refused.</title>
        <text class="nl2-kicker" x="2" y="10">Host key</text>

        <rect class="nl2-row" x="2" y="18" width="256" height="28" rx="2" />
        <circle class="nl2-dot" cx="18" cy="32" r="3.5" />
        <text class="nl2-state" x="32" y="36">a new key after a wipe</text>
        <text class="nl2-verdict" x="250" y="36" text-anchor="end">expected</text>

        <rect class="nl2-row" x="2" y="50" width="256" height="28" rx="2" />
        <circle class="nl2-dot" cx="18" cy="64" r="3.5" />
        <text class="nl2-state" x="32" y="68">the same key</text>
        <text class="nl2-verdict" x="250" y="68" text-anchor="end">known</text>

        <rect class="nl2-row-marked" x="2" y="82" width="256" height="28" rx="2" />
        <circle class="nl2-dot-marked" cx="18" cy="96" r="4" />
        <text class="nl2-state" x="32" y="100">a key that changed mid-cycle</text>
        <text class="nl2-mark-label" x="250" y="100" text-anchor="end">Refuse</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">Netinstall regenerates the host keys on every
          cycle, so a new key is the normal case and not a warning. A
          lab-scoped known-hosts file discards one file per cycle and still
          refuses the one state that means something is wrong.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">It does not show the passphrase prompt, which
          stops a script too and is a separate problem.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The device end is unattended. What remains is host-side automation hygiene
      on the machine that drives it: a lab-only known-hosts file, and a key the
      script can use without a prompt. Those two would have blocked every later
      piece of automation in this lab.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="three-back-doors-one-shape">Three back doors, one shape</h2>
    <p>
      The eventual experiment asks whether an agent on a live router can avoid a
      self-lockout. That question is only meaningful if a lockout is actually
      possible. So the provisioning config has to be deliberate about every path
      into the device, and not just the obvious one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Pi reaches the router through exactly one firewall rule: SSH, from one
      address, on one interface, above a deny-everything-else rule. That rule is
      the surface the experiment is meant to attack. I found three other ways
      in, and they are all the same shape: a management path that survives the
      failure under study.
    </p>

    <!-- One intended path in, and the three side paths I found later. Each
         side path carries its own disposition, and the one that was open is
         marked. The map carries the set; the reasoning stays as prose in the
         list below it. Accent marks the open path. Oxide means a reversal on
         this site and nothing here is a reversal, so oxide does not appear
         (the same rule .lab-topo records above). No digit renders here, and
         that is deliberate. IPv6 and layer-2 each carry a digit, so the plate
         says link-local and the prose below keeps both names in full. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="map" data-place="sheet">
      <span class="fig-label">Map 03</span>
      <svg class="nl1-doors" viewBox="0 0 260 150" role="img"
           aria-label="The paths into the router. The intended path is SSH, the path under test. I found three side paths later. MAC-Winbox is restricted to the LAN side. Link-local is open by default. RoMON is off by default. Only the link-local path was open.">
        <title>The paths into the router. The intended path is SSH, the path under test. I found three side paths later. MAC-Winbox is restricted to the LAN side. Link-local is open by default. RoMON is off by default. Only the link-local path was open.</title>
        <defs>
          <marker id="nl1-head" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
          <marker id="nl1-head-open" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path class="nl1-head-open" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <rect class="nl1-box" x="192" y="16" width="64" height="124" rx="2" />
        <text class="nl1-id" x="224" y="82" text-anchor="middle">Router</text>

        <text class="nl1-group" x="2" y="10">Intended</text>
        <text class="nl1-name" x="2" y="24">SSH</text>
        <text class="nl1-disp" x="2" y="36">the path under test</text>
        <path class="nl1-edge" d="M 118 28 L 188 28" marker-end="url(#nl1-head)" />

        <text class="nl1-group" x="2" y="56">Found later</text>

        <text class="nl1-name" x="2" y="70">MAC-Winbox</text>
        <text class="nl1-disp" x="2" y="82">LAN side only</text>
        <path class="nl1-edge" d="M 118 74 L 168 74" />
        <path class="nl1-stop" d="M 172 67 L 172 81" />

        <text class="nl1-name" x="2" y="98">link-local</text>
        <text class="nl1-open-label" x="66" y="98">Open</text>
        <text class="nl1-disp" x="2" y="110">open by default</text>
        <circle class="nl1-open-mark" cx="118" cy="102" r="4" />
        <path class="nl1-open" d="M 122 102 L 188 102" marker-end="url(#nl1-head-open)" />

        <text class="nl1-name" x="2" y="126">RoMON</text>
        <text class="nl1-disp" x="2" y="138">off by default</text>
        <path class="nl1-edge" d="M 118 130 L 168 130" />
        <path class="nl1-stop" d="M 172 123 L 172 137" />
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">Three side paths share one shape with the path
          under test: each survives the failure the experiment studies.
          MAC-Winbox and RoMON are closed. Link-local is open by default, so a
          failed lockout would have produced a wrong answer in silence.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">It does not show that the enumeration of paths
          has no obvious end. That argument stays in the prose.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>MAC-Winbox.</strong> This is MikroTik&rsquo;s layer-2
        management, and it works with no IP configured anywhere. I left it
        restricted to the LAN side. The Pi therefore has no layer-2 fallback and
        can genuinely lock itself out. My desktop, on the other side, keeps it
        and stays the recovery post.</li>
      <li><strong>IPv6 link-local.</strong> This is the one I nearly missed.
        RouterOS brings up link-local IPv6 automatically, and my config had no
        IPv6 firewall rules at all. So IPv6 input stayed open by default. An
        agent could have destroyed every IPv4 path I narrowed so carefully, and
        the Pi would still have connected over IPv6. That failure would not have
        looked like a broken experiment. It would have quietly produced a wrong
        answer.</li>
      <li><strong>RoMON.</strong> This is another layer-2 overlay, and I found
        it while I read an unrelated diagnostic. It is off by default on this
        device. A restrictive shipping mode helped me here, and I did not ask
        for it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      I found the second path when I applied the question from the first. I
      found the third by accident. <strong>&ldquo;Locked out&rdquo; only means
      something after an enumeration of every independent path in</strong>, and
      there is no obvious point at which that enumeration is finished.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="a-deferred-item-that-turned-out-not-to-be">A deferred item that turned out not to be deferrable</h2>
    <p>
      RouterOS has a feature called device-mode, and it gates whole categories
      of functionality. A change to device-mode requires physical confirmation:
      a power cycle within a five-minute window. You cannot make that change
      over SSH alone, which is precisely the point of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I recorded device-mode as relevant to the <em>later</em> experiment, and I
      filed it accordingly. It then blocked the current one. The command that
      arms the router for its next wipe cycle is gated behind a device-mode
      flag. A factory board ships with that flag off. So the provisioning
      script&rsquo;s final line could not run on a fresh device. No script could
      fix that, because the resolution requires someone to physically pull a
      plug.
    </p>
    <p>
      &ldquo;This only affects a later phase&rdquo; was an assumption I recorded
      as though it were a finding. It was about the one subsystem whose entire
      purpose is to block things. The decision record now carries the correction
      in place rather than a tidy rewrite.
    </p>

    <!-- The dependency chain, drawn once. It runs one way and it ends in a
         node no script can enter, which is the whole reason a deferred item
         stopped the current build. The accent box and the filled mark plus
         BY HAND reuse the .lab-topo hand encoding above, so one page states
         "a person is required" in one grammar. No digit renders here: the
         first node is the script's last line, and ol.cycle keeps the step
