    <p>
      The flag survives a reinstall. I therefore pay to enable it one time for
      each device, and not on every cycle. That answer also kept a RouterOS
      version upgrade off the critical path. A flag that reset would have put
      that upgrade on the critical path.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="what-this-does-not-establish">What this does not establish</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>This is not standards-based ZTP.</strong> Real zero-touch
        provisioning has a trust story: RFC 8572, BRSKI, a certificate
        authority, and a device that proves what it is before it accepts
        configuration. This has none of that. The trust model is that I plugged
        in the cable myself. It is the operational mechanics of unattended
        provisioning without the security substrate that makes it safe at
        scale.</li>
      <li><strong>One device, one model, one OS version.</strong> A handful of
        cycles on a single board. Nothing here is a claim about how MikroTik
        hardware behaves in general. Nothing here is a claim about any other
        maker&rsquo;s hardware.</li>
      <li><strong>Unattended within a session, not across a reboot.</strong> The
        provisioning host holds its SSH key in an agent that a human has to
        unlock after the Pi restarts. I made that trade deliberately against a
        passphrase-free key. It affects the experiment this lab exists for,
        because an unattended lab is exactly the scenario where a reboot is most
        likely.</li>
      <li><strong>One thing is unresolved.</strong> The setting that
        re-arms the router lives in the bootloader, not the OS. It may have
        survived the reinstall on its own, and not from a re-application by my
        script. Both produce identical output. This board is armed either way. A
        truly factory board might not be armed, and I have not yet run the test
        that distinguishes them.</li>
      <li><strong>The actual experiment is not built.</strong> The self-lockout
        question is the reason this lab exists, and everything above is the
        apparatus for it.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <p>
      MikroTik hEX (RouterOS 7, ARM), Raspberry Pi 5 with Raspberry Pi OS
      on NVMe, MikroTik Netinstall under <code>qemu-user-static</code>, BOOTP
      and TFTP on an isolated point-to-point link, RouterOS scripting for
      the provisioning configuration, and a shell wrapper so a full cycle is one
      command. The repo is public:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/netops-lab">github.com/sanlee-ys/netops-lab</a>.
      It carries the provisioning script, the decision records, and a field log
      of what failed and what the symptoms actually meant. All of them are
      checkable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The field log is as detailed as the design docs. Two things cost the most
      time in this build. The first was an Ethernet link that would not come up
      until I disabled a power-saving feature on the host network card. The
      second was an interface that reported a perfectly correct IP address while
      no cable was attached to it.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="what-it-demonstrates">What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      I built the recovery mechanism before the experiment that needs it. The
      forced-password-change problem would have taken a day to solve properly,
      and it did not exist. One command established that. The IPv6 hole would
      have taken no time at all to leave in place, and it would have invalidated
      the results without a visible failure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of what I do professionally is measured on software I can re-run
      until it is right. Hardware is less forgiving about that. A management
      plane you reach through is less forgiving still. Every change is
      potentially the change that ends the session. That is why the lab
      exists.
    </p>
